+---
+id: task-005-implement-createpatch-json-patch-rfc-6902-generation
+type: task
+title: "Implement createPatch: JSON Patch (RFC 6902) generation"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Implement createPatch: JSON Patch (RFC 6902) generation
+
+Our clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the whole
+new version. Produce such a change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902). It is the inverse of `applyPatch` (task-002
+and task-003): applying the produced patch to `from` gives `to`.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Export a function `createPatch(from, to)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns a `JsonPatch` that turns `from`
+  into `to` when `applyPatch` applies it: `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` equals `to`.
+- The patch describes what changed, and nothing more:
+  - Equal values give an empty patch.
+  - Two objects are compared member by member: a member only in `from` is removed; a member only in `to`
+    is added; a member in both is compared recursively, so a small change deep inside is a small operation
+    at its own path, not a replacement of the enclosing document.
+  - Two arrays are compared element by element, with an alignment of equal elements (for example an LCS over
+    deep equality). An element added or removed is one `add` or `remove` at its index; the elements around it
+    are not rewritten. An element that moved is removed and added back (allowed by the request); the elements
+    between are left alone.
+  - Where unmatched elements of `from` and `to` sit in the same gap, they are paired by position: a pair of
+    objects or arrays is compared recursively, so a changed element gives a small patch inside it.
+  - A value whose type differs (object, array, scalar or `null`), or a scalar that changed, is a `replace`.
+    When the root itself differs in that way, the operation's path is `""`.
+- The patch must not mutate `from` or `to`, and must share no object with them: a value in an `add` or
+  `replace` is a copy.
+- Pointers are escaped as RFC 6901 requires: `~` as `~0` and `/` as `~1`, with `~` escaped first.
+- Throw an error when `from` or `to` is not a JSON value, as `applyPatch` does for its document.
+- The array alignment must stay tractable: trim the common prefix and suffix first; when the middle is too
+  large for the alignment table, fall back to pairing by position. The result stays correct in both cases.
+- No runtime dependency is added; `src/index.ts` keeps its existing exports and public types.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- Tests are written first and fail for the missing behaviour before the implementation (red-first, since
+  the behaviour is new).
+- Every behaviour has at least one happy-path test and one edge- or error-path test.
+- Tests cover: equal inputs give `[]`; a changed scalar; a root type change giving `path ""`; a member added,
+  removed, and changed deep inside; an array append, a removal in the middle, and an insertion in the middle,
+  each giving exactly one operation and touching no other element; a moved element giving a small patch that
+  does not rewrite the elements between; a small change inside an element of an array giving a nested path;
+  keys containing `/` and `~`; a `__proto__` member; a round-trip check `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))`
+  equal to `to` over the cases above and over a seeded set of random documents.
+- Tests cover that neither input is mutated and that the patch shares no object with the inputs.
+- Tests cover rejection of a non-JSON `from` or `to`.
+- `npm test` passes, with the existing tests still passing.
+
+## Notes
+
+- Reuse `isJsonValue`, `jsonEqual`, `setMember` and `isJsonObject` from `src/index.ts`; `escapeToken` is
+  the one place that builds reference tokens for `createPatch`.
+- `applyPatch` already accepts `add` at index `length`, `replace` at path `""`, and `__proto__` as a member.
+
+## Outcome
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` is exported from `src/index.ts`, after `applyMergePatch`. It returns a `JsonPatch`
+that `applyPatch` turns from `from` into `to`:
+
+- Objects are diffed member by member: a removed member is a `remove`, an added one an `add`, and a member
+  in both is diffed recursively. A type change or a changed scalar is a `replace`, at path `""` for the root.
+- Arrays are aligned by their common prefix and suffix, then by the longest common subsequence of equal
+  elements. An added or removed element is one `add` or `remove` at its index; a moved element is removed
+  and added back; the elements between are not rewritten. Unaligned elements in the same gap are paired by
+  position and diffed recursively, so a small change inside an element is a nested operation.
+- When the middle of an array would need more than 1,000,000 alignment cells, the middle is not aligned: it
+  is paired by position. The result stays correct, but may be larger. A 1,500-element array with a few
+  changes gives 149 operations and round-trips, in about 40 ms.
+- Keys are escaped (`~` as `~0`, then `/` as `~1`). Values in `add` and `replace` are copies, so the patch
+  shares no object with the inputs, and neither input is mutated.
+
+Tests: 18 new cases in `src/index.test.ts`, written first and failing with the export missing. They cover
+equal inputs, scalars, root type change, object members, array insert, remove and move, nested changes,
+escaped keys, `__proto__`, a round trip over fixed cases and 300 seeded random pairs, non-mutation and
+aliasing, and rejection of non-JSON inputs. `npm test` runs 77 tests, all passing.
+
+No TypeScript compiler is installed in this environment, so the strict type check of `tsconfig.json` was
+not run; the tests run the TypeScript source through Node's type stripping.
